/* LexSophia TEAP Listening — Set 3 bank (50 questions). Loaded by teap/listening; selected via the launcher. */
window.TEAP_LISTENING_SETS = window.TEAP_LISTENING_SETS || {};
window.TEAP_LISTENING_SETS[3] = {"p1":[{"id":"L-1A-01","num":1,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が音楽教室でギターのレッスンについて話しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Hi, I'm interested in guitar lessons. Do you have classes for complete beginners?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Yes, our beginner course meets on Wednesday evenings and Saturday mornings.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Wednesday evenings work for me. Do I need my own guitar?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"You can rent one here for the first month, but most students buy their own after that.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Then I'll rent one for now and join the Wednesday class.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Great. The first lesson is next Wednesday at seven. Please arrive fifteen minutes early for the rental paperwork.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-t6.mp3"}],"question":"What will the woman do at the music school?","options":["Buy a guitar this week","Join the Saturday morning class","Rent a guitar and join the Wednesday class","Take a trial lesson on Wednesday morning"],"answer":2,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-01-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：こんにちは、ギターのレッスンに興味があります。完全な初心者向けのクラスはありますか？\n男：はい、初心者コースは水曜の夜と土曜の朝にあります。\n女：水曜の夜なら大丈夫です。自分のギターが必要ですか？\n男：最初の1か月はここでレンタルできますが、その後は大抵の生徒さんが自分のを買います。\n女：では当分レンタルして、水曜のクラスに入ります。\n男：いいですね。初回は来週水曜の19時です。レンタルの手続きのため15分早めに来てください。","evidence":"I'll rent one for now and join the Wednesday class.","explanation":"女性はギターをレンタルし、水曜夜の初心者クラスに入ることにした。","trapType":"選択肢の組み合わせ型"},{"id":"L-1A-02","num":2,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が図書館でグループ学習室の予約について話しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Hi, I reserved study room four for this afternoon, but our group has grown to eight people. The room only seats six.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Room seven seats ten, and it is free this afternoon. Shall I move your reservation?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Yes, please. Is the time still two to four?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Room seven is booked until two thirty, so your session would be two thirty to four thirty.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"That's fine. Our presentation practice can start a little later.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Done. Room seven, from two thirty to four thirty today.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-t6.mp3"}],"question":"When will the group use the study room?","options":["From two to four in room four","From two thirty to four thirty in room seven","From two to four in room seven","From two thirty to four thirty in room four"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-02-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：こんにちは、今日の午後に学習室4を予約したのですが、グループが8人になってしまって。その部屋は6人までなんです。\n女：学習室7は10人入れますし、今日の午後は空いています。予約を移しましょうか？\n男：はい、お願いします。時間は14時から16時のままですか？\n女：学習室7は14時半まで予約が入っているので、14時半から16時半になります。\n男：大丈夫です。発表練習は少し遅く始められます。\n女：完了しました。今日、学習室7を14時半から16時半です。","evidence":"Room seven, from two thirty to four thirty today.","explanation":"部屋が7に変わり、時間も14:30–16:30にずれた。部屋と時間の組み合わせで判断する。","trapType":"部屋と時間の両方が変わる型"},{"id":"L-1A-03","num":3,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が寮の管理人にエアコンの騒音について話しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Excuse me, the air conditioner in my room has been making a loud noise since last night.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I'm sorry about that. Is it a rattling sound, or more like a hum?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"It's a rattling sound, and it gets worse when I turn the fan up.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"That usually means a loose part inside. I'll send the repair staff tomorrow morning. Will you be in your room at ten?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"I have class at ten, but my roommate will be there. I'll tell her to let them in.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Perfect. If the noise gets worse tonight, please call the front desk and we will lend you a fan.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-t6.mp3"}],"question":"What will happen tomorrow morning?","options":["The woman will move to a new room","Repair staff will come to the woman's room","The woman will buy a fan","The manager will inspect another room"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-03-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：すみません、私の部屋のエアコンが昨夜から大きな音を立てています。\n男：それは申し訳ありません。ガタガタという音ですか、それともブーンという音ですか？\n女：ガタガタという音で、ファンを強くするとひどくなります。\n男：それは通常、内部の部品の緩みです。明日の朝、修理スタッフを向かわせます。10時に部屋にいますか？\n女：10時は授業がありますが、ルームメイトがいます。入れるように伝えておきます。\n男：完璧です。今夜音がひどくなったら、フロントに電話してください。扇風機をお貸しします。","evidence":"I'll send the repair staff tomorrow morning.","explanation":"明日の朝に修理スタッフが部屋に来る。本人は授業で不在だがルームメイトが対応する。","trapType":"当事者が不在でも進む型"},{"id":"L-1A-04","num":4,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が花屋で卒業式の花を買っています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Hi, I'd like a bouquet for my friend's graduation tomorrow.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Congratulations to your friend. Our tulip bouquet is popular for graduations, at 3,000 yen.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"That sounds nice, but my budget is 2,000 yen. What can I get for that?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"For 2,000 yen, I can make a smaller bouquet with tulips and some white roses. It will still look full.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Please do that. Can I pick it up tomorrow morning?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Yes, we open at nine. I will have it ready by then, with a message card.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-t6.mp3"}],"question":"What will the man buy?","options":["A tulip bouquet for 3,000 yen","A smaller bouquet with tulips and white roses","Only white roses","A bouquet without a message card"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-04-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：こんにちは、明日の友達の卒業式用に花束が欲しいです。\n女：お友達、おめでとうございます。チューリップの花束は卒業式に人気で、3,000円です。\n男：いいですね。でも予算は2,000円なんです。その値段だと何が買えますか？\n女：2,000円なら、チューリップと白いバラで小さめの花束を作れます。見た目は十分華やかですよ。\n男：それでお願いします。明日の朝受け取れますか？\n女：はい、9時開店です。その時間までに、メッセージカード付きで用意しておきます。","evidence":"For 2,000 yen, I can make a smaller bouquet with tulips and some white roses.","explanation":"予算2,000円に合わせ、チューリップと白バラの小さめの花束になった。","trapType":"予算で内容が変わる型"},{"id":"L-1A-05","num":5,"part":"1A","situation":"学生がクラスメートにエッセイの添削を頼んでいます。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Ken, could you read my essay before Friday? It's for the English writing class.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Sure. How long is it?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"About 800 words. I mainly want you to check the grammar, especially the articles — I always mix up 'a' and 'the.'","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"No problem. But I have a part-time job on Thursday night, so send it by Wednesday evening.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"I'll send it tonight, then. In return, I'll check the spelling in your presentation slides.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Deal. I'll return your essay with comments on Thursday morning.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-t6.mp3"}],"question":"What does the woman especially want the man to check?","options":["The length of the essay","The use of articles","The spelling","The conclusion"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-05-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：ケン、金曜までに私のエッセイを読んでくれない？英語ライティングの授業のものなの。\n男：いいよ。どのくらいの長さ？\n女：800語くらい。主に文法を見てほしいんだけど、特に冠詞。aとtheをいつも混同しちゃうの。\n男：大丈夫。でも木曜の夜はバイトがあるから、水曜の夜までに送って。\n女：じゃあ今夜送るね。お返しに、あなたの発表スライドのスペルをチェックするよ。\n男：決まり。木曜の朝にコメント付きでエッセイを返すよ。","evidence":"I mainly want you to check the grammar, especially the articles","explanation":"特に見てほしいのは冠詞（a/the）の使い方。スペルチェックは女性が男性に提供する方。","trapType":"頼む側と引き受ける側の混同"},{"id":"L-1A-06","num":6,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が薬局で風邪薬について相談しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Excuse me, I have a sore throat and a slight fever. What medicine do you recommend?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"This one works for both throat pain and fever. Take one tablet three times a day, after meals.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Does it make you sleepy? I have to drive this afternoon.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Yes, it can cause drowsiness. For driving days, this other type is safer — it treats the fever but not the throat.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Then I'll take the second one today, and use throat lozenges for my throat.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Good choice. And please rest and drink plenty of water.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-t6.mp3"}],"question":"Why does the man choose the second medicine?","options":["It is cheaper than the first","It does not cause drowsiness","It treats both fever and throat pain","It works faster than the first"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-06-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：すみません、喉が痛くて少し熱があります。どの薬がいいですか？\n女：こちらは喉の痛みと熱の両方に効きます。1回1錠を1日3回、食後に飲んでください。\n男：眠くなりますか？今日の午後、運転しなければならなくて。\n女：はい、眠気が出ることがあります。運転する日は、こちらの別タイプが安全です。熱には効きますが喉には効きません。\n男：では今日は2番目のものにして、喉にはのど飴を使います。\n女：いい選択です。あと、休んで水をたくさん飲んでくださいね。","evidence":"Yes, it can cause drowsiness. For driving days, this other type is safer","explanation":"午後運転するため、眠気の出る最初の薬を避けて2番目を選んだ。","trapType":"副作用による選択変更"},{"id":"L-1A-07","num":7,"part":"1A","situation":"2人の友人が旅行の夜行バスについて話しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"For our trip to Kyoto, the night bus is much cheaper than the train. It leaves at eleven at night and arrives at six thirty.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Arriving at six thirty is early, but we can leave our bags at the hotel and start sightseeing.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"There are two seat types. Regular seats are 6,000 yen, and the wider seats with more legroom are 8,500 yen.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"It's a seven-hour ride. I think the wider seats are worth it for sleeping.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Agreed. I'll book two wide seats by the window — no, wait, one window and one aisle, so we can get up easily.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Good thinking. Please book before Friday, because the weekend buses fill up quickly.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-t6.mp3"}],"question":"Which seats will the woman book?","options":["Two regular window seats","Two wide seats, one by the window and one on the aisle","Two wide window seats","One regular and one wide seat"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-07-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：京都旅行は、夜行バスが電車よりずっと安いよ。夜11時に出て6時半に着く。\n男：6時半着は早いけど、ホテルに荷物を置いて観光を始められるね。\n女：座席は2種類ある。通常席が6,000円で、足元が広いワイド席が8,500円。\n男：7時間の乗車だから、眠るためにはワイド席が価値あると思う。\n女：賛成。窓側2席を予約するね――あ、待って、窓側1席と通路側1席にしよう。立ちやすいから。\n男：いい考え。週末のバスはすぐ埋まるから、金曜までに予約してね。","evidence":"I'll book two wide seats by the window — no, wait, one window and one aisle","explanation":"ワイド席2席で、言い直しにより「窓側1＋通路側1」になった。自己訂正の聞き取りが鍵。","trapType":"自己訂正型"},{"id":"L-1A-08","num":8,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が図書館でカメラの貸出について聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Hi, I heard students can borrow a camera from the library. Is that true?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Yes, we lend digital cameras for class projects. The loan period is three days.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I need one for a photography assignment this weekend. Can I reserve it for Friday?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Friday is possible. But you must return it by Monday noon, and there is a late fee of 500 yen per day.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"That's fine. Do I need anything besides my student ID?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Please also bring a note from your professor confirming it is for a class project.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-t6.mp3"}],"question":"What must the man bring to borrow the camera?","options":["Only his student ID","His student ID and a note from his professor","A 500 yen deposit","His own memory card"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-08-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：こんにちは、学生は図書館でカメラを借りられると聞いたのですが、本当ですか？\n女：はい、授業のプロジェクト用にデジタルカメラを貸し出しています。貸出期間は3日です。\n男：今週末、写真の課題で必要なのですが、金曜に予約できますか？\n女：金曜は可能です。ただし月曜の正午までに返却が必要で、延滞料は1日500円です。\n男：大丈夫です。学生証のほかに何か必要ですか？\n女：授業のプロジェクト用であることを確認する、先生からのメモもお持ちください。","evidence":"Please also bring a note from your professor confirming it is for a class project.","explanation":"学生証に加え、教授のメモが必要。500円は預かり金ではなく延滞料。","trapType":"金額の意味の取り違え"},{"id":"L-1A-09","num":9,"part":"1A","situation":"学生がビーチ清掃ボランティアの集合場所について聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Hi, you're signed up for Saturday's beach cleanup, right? I wanted to tell you the meeting point has changed.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Oh, I thought we were meeting at the station at nine.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"That was the original plan, but the bus stop is under construction. Now we'll meet at the beach entrance at nine thirty instead.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I see. Should I bring anything?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Just gloves if you have them. We provide bags, water, and sunscreen.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Got it. Beach entrance, nine thirty, with gloves.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-t6.mp3"}],"question":"Where and when will the volunteers meet?","options":["At the station at nine","At the beach entrance at nine thirty","At the bus stop at nine thirty","At the beach entrance at nine"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-09-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：こんにちは、土曜のビーチ清掃に申し込んでいますよね。集合場所が変わったのでお伝えしたくて。\n男：あれ、9時に駅集合だと思っていました。\n女：それが元の計画だったんですが、バス停が工事中なんです。今は代わりに9時半にビーチの入り口集合です。\n男：分かりました。何か持っていくものはありますか？\n女：手袋があればそれだけ。袋と水と日焼け止めはこちらで用意します。\n男：了解です。ビーチの入り口に9時半、手袋を持って。","evidence":"Now we'll meet at the beach entrance at nine thirty instead.","explanation":"バス停の工事のため、集合は駅9時からビーチ入口9時半に変更された。","trapType":"変更前後の混同"},{"id":"L-1A-10","num":10,"part":"1A","situation":"学生が学食のアルバイトについてマネージャーと話しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Hi, I'm here about the part-time job in the cafeteria. Which shifts are available?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"We need people for the lunch shift, eleven to two, and the dinner shift, five to eight.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I have classes until twelve thirty, so the lunch shift is impossible. The dinner shift works, though.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Can you work three evenings a week?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Yes, Monday, Wednesday, and Friday evenings are free.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Then you can start next Monday. Training takes place during your first two shifts.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-t6.mp3"}],"question":"Which shift will the man work?","options":["The lunch shift on Mondays","The dinner shift three evenings a week","The lunch shift three days a week","The dinner shift every evening"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1a-10-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：こんにちは、学食のアルバイトについて来ました。空いているシフトはどれですか？\n女：ランチシフト（11時〜14時）とディナーシフト（17時〜20時）で人を探しています。\n男：授業が12時半まであるので、ランチシフトは無理です。ディナーシフトなら大丈夫です。\n女：週3回の夜は働けますか？\n男：はい、月曜・水曜・金曜の夜は空いています。\n女：では来週の月曜から始められます。研修は最初の2シフトの間に行います。","evidence":"I have classes until twelve thirty, so the lunch shift is impossible. The dinner shift works, though.","explanation":"授業のためランチシフトは不可で、週3回（月・水・金）のディナーシフトに決まった。","trapType":"制約によるシフト決定"},{"id":"L-1B-11","num":11,"part":"1B","situation":"美術館からのお知らせを聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"The City Art Museum will hold its Student Day on Sunday, April 20th. On that day, all university and high school students can enter the museum free of charge by showing a student ID at the entrance. The special exhibition of French paintings, which normally costs an extra 800 yen, is also included. Guided tours will be offered at eleven and two, each lasting about forty minutes, with no reservation needed. Please note that the museum café will be closed for renovation that day, and photography is allowed only in the permanent exhibition rooms, not in the special exhibition.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-11-t1.mp3"}],"question":"What is true about Student Day?","options":["Students must reserve the guided tours in advance","The special exhibition costs an extra 800 yen that day","Photography is allowed everywhere in the museum","Students can enter free, including the special exhibition"],"answer":3,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-11-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：市立美術館は4月20日（日）に学生デーを開催します。当日は、大学生と高校生全員が、入口で学生証を見せれば無料で入館できます。通常は別途800円かかるフランス絵画の特別展も含まれます。ガイドツアーは11時と14時に行われ、各回約40分、予約不要です。なお、当日は美術館のカフェが改装のため閉まっており、写真撮影は常設展の部屋でのみ可能で、特別展ではできません。","evidence":"all university and high school students can enter the museum free of charge by showing a student ID","explanation":"学生デーは入館無料で特別展も含まれる。ツアーは予約不要、撮影は常設展のみ。","trapType":"正誤判定（複数情報の照合）"},{"id":"L-1B-12","num":12,"part":"1B","situation":"GPSの仕組みについての短い講義を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Most of us use GPS every day, but how does it actually find our position? The system depends on about thirty satellites orbiting the Earth. Each satellite constantly sends out signals that include the exact time the signal was sent. Your phone receives signals from several satellites and measures how long each signal took to arrive. Since the signals travel at the speed of light, the phone can calculate its distance from each satellite. With signals from at least four satellites, the phone can determine its exact position. The system was originally developed for the military, but it was opened to the public in the 1980s and 1990s.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-12-t1.mp3"}],"question":"How does a phone calculate its position using GPS?","options":["By sending signals back to the satellites","By measuring how long satellite signals take to arrive","By connecting to the nearest phone tower","By taking photos of the ground"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-12-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：私たちの多くは毎日GPSを使いますが、実際にはどうやって位置を特定しているのでしょうか。このシステムは、地球を周回する約30基の衛星に依存しています。各衛星は、送信された正確な時刻を含む信号を常時送っています。あなたの電話は複数の衛星から信号を受け取り、それぞれの信号が到着するまでの時間を測定します。信号は光速で進むため、電話は各衛星からの距離を計算できます。少なくとも4基の衛星からの信号があれば、電話は正確な位置を特定できます。このシステムはもともと軍事用に開発されましたが、1980年代から90年代に一般公開されました。","evidence":"measures how long each signal took to arrive","explanation":"信号の到達時間を測り、光速から距離を計算するのがGPSの原理。最低4基の衛星が必要。","trapType":"仕組みの聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1B-13","num":13,"part":"1B","situation":"映画研究入門の授業紹介を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Welcome to Introduction to Film Studies. In this course, we will watch one film each week and analyze how it was made. The first half of the course covers classic Hollywood films, and the second half introduces films from Japan, France, and India. You do not need any background in film, but you must watch each week's film before class, because our discussions assume everyone has seen it. Films are available at the university media center. Your final grade will be based on two short essays and one group presentation in which you analyze a scene of your choice in detail. There will be no written exam.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-13-t1.mp3"}],"question":"What must students do before each class?","options":["Read a chapter about the film","Watch that week's film","Write a short essay","Visit the media center with their group"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-13-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：映画研究入門へようこそ。この授業では毎週1本の映画を見て、その作り方を分析します。前半は古典的なハリウッド映画を扱い、後半は日本、フランス、インドの映画を紹介します。映画の背景知識は必要ありませんが、議論は全員が見たことを前提とするため、各週の映画を授業前に必ず見てください。映画は大学のメディアセンターで見られます。最終成績は短いエッセイ2本と、好きな場面を詳しく分析するグループ発表1回で決まります。筆記試験はありません。","evidence":"you must watch each week's film before class","explanation":"授業前の必須事項は、その週の映画を観ること。エッセイは成績評価の一部。","trapType":"義務と評価項目の区別"},{"id":"L-1B-14","num":14,"part":"1B","situation":"大学シャトルバスの路線変更のお知らせを聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"This is an announcement about the campus shuttle bus. From next Monday, the bus route will change for about two months because of construction on the road in front of the main gate. During this period, the bus will not stop at the main gate. Instead, please use the temporary stop near the east gate, which is a three-minute walk from the main gate. The timetable will stay the same, with buses every fifteen minutes. The last bus from the station will still leave at nine forty-five at night. We will announce the return to the normal route on the university website as soon as the construction is finished.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-14-t1.mp3"}],"question":"What change will the construction cause?","options":["Buses will run every thirty minutes","The bus will stop at the east gate instead of the main gate","The last bus will leave earlier","The shuttle service will be suspended for two months"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-14-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：キャンパスシャトルバスについてのお知らせです。来週の月曜から、正門前の道路工事のため、約2か月間バスの路線が変更されます。この期間、バスは正門には停まりません。代わりに、正門から徒歩3分の東門近くの臨時停留所をご利用ください。時刻表は変わらず、15分ごとにバスが出ます。駅からの最終バスは今まで通り夜9時45分発です。工事が終わり次第、通常路線への復帰を大学のウェブサイトでお知らせします。","evidence":"the bus will not stop at the main gate. Instead, please use the temporary stop near the east gate","explanation":"変更点は停留所の移転（正門→東門の臨時停留所）のみで、時刻や間隔は不変。","trapType":"変更点と不変点の区別"},{"id":"L-1B-15","num":15,"part":"1B","situation":"日本におけるお茶の歴史についての短い講義を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Tea first came to Japan from China in the ninth century, brought by Buddhist monks. At first, it was a rare and expensive drink used mainly in temples. In the twelfth century, the monk Eisai introduced powdered green tea and wrote the first Japanese book about tea, arguing that it was good for health. During the sixteenth century, the tea ceremony developed under masters such as Sen no Rikyu, who valued simplicity and quiet beauty. Only in the Edo period did tea become a drink for ordinary people, as farming methods improved and prices fell. By the nineteenth century, Japanese green tea was being exported to America and Europe in large quantities.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-15-t1.mp3"}],"question":"When did tea become a drink for ordinary people in Japan?","options":["In the ninth century","In the twelfth century","In the sixteenth century","In the Edo period"],"answer":3,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-15-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：お茶は9世紀に中国から仏教の僧侶によって日本に初めて伝わりました。最初は希少で高価な飲み物で、主に寺院で使われました。12世紀には、僧の栄西が粉末の緑茶を伝え、日本初の茶の本を書いて、健康に良いと説きました。16世紀には、千利休のような師匠のもとで茶道が発展し、簡素さと静かな美しさが重んじられました。お茶が普通の人々の飲み物になったのは、栽培方法が改良され価格が下がった江戸時代になってからです。19世紀までには、日本の緑茶がアメリカやヨーロッパに大量に輸出されていました。","evidence":"Only in the Edo period did tea become a drink for ordinary people","explanation":"庶民の飲み物になったのは江戸時代。各世紀の出来事との対応を聞き分ける。","trapType":"年代と出来事の対応"},{"id":"L-1B-16","num":16,"part":"1B","situation":"人間の目が色を見る仕組みについての短い講義を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Why do we see the world in color? Inside the human eye, there are two kinds of light-sensitive cells. Rod cells work in dim light but cannot distinguish colors, which is why everything looks gray at night. Cone cells, by contrast, work in bright light and give us color vision. Most people have three types of cone cells: one sensitive mainly to red light, one to green, and one to blue. The brain combines the signals from these three types to create all the colors we see. Interestingly, some animals have four types, and scientists believe they may see colors that humans cannot even imagine.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-16-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Why does everything look gray at night?","options":["The cone cells become more active","Only rod cells work in dim light, and they cannot distinguish colors","The brain stops combining signals","The eyes have fewer cells at night"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-16-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：なぜ私たちは世界を色付きで見るのでしょうか。人間の目の中には、光を感じる細胞が2種類あります。桿体細胞は暗い光で働きますが色を区別できないため、夜はすべてが灰色に見えます。一方、錐体細胞は明るい光で働き、色覚を与えてくれます。ほとんどの人は3種類の錐体細胞を持ち、1つは主に赤い光に、1つは緑に、1つは青に敏感です。脳はこの3種類からの信号を組み合わせて、私たちが見える全ての色を作ります。興味深いことに、動物の中には4種類を持つものがいて、科学者は、彼らが人間には想像もできない色を見ているかもしれないと考えています。","evidence":"Rod cells work in dim light but cannot distinguish colors, which is why everything looks gray at night.","explanation":"夜に灰色に見えるのは、暗所で働く桿体細胞が色を区別できないから。","trapType":"理由の直接聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1B-17","num":17,"part":"1B","situation":"国際交流写真コンテストの案内を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"The International Office is pleased to announce this year's photo contest for students. The theme is 'My Second Home': photographs that show a place outside your home country that feels like home to you. Each student may enter up to two photographs, taken within the last two years. The deadline for entries is October 31st, and entries are submitted through the university website. The winning photographs will be displayed in the library entrance hall in December. The first prize is a 30,000 yen book voucher. Exchange students and Japanese students who have studied abroad are both eligible to enter.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-17-t1.mp3"}],"question":"What is the theme of the photo contest?","options":["Beautiful places in Japan","My Second Home","Life on campus","Travel memories with friends"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-17-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：国際課は、今年の学生向け写真コンテストの開催をお知らせします。テーマは「私の第二の故郷」で、母国以外で故郷のように感じる場所を写した写真です。各学生は、過去2年以内に撮影した写真を2点まで応募できます。締切は10月31日で、応募は大学のウェブサイトから行います。入賞作品は12月に図書館のエントランスホールに展示されます。最優秀賞は3万円分の図書券です。留学生も、留学経験のある日本人学生も応募できます。","evidence":"The theme is 'My Second Home'","explanation":"テーマは「My Second Home（私の第二の故郷）」。締切や賞品と混同しないこと。","trapType":"テーマの直接聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1B-18","num":18,"part":"1B","situation":"ペンギンと南極研究についての短い講義を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Penguins are often used as symbols of Antarctica, and they are also valuable research subjects for scientists. Because penguins depend on sea ice for breeding and for hunting the small fish and shrimp beneath it, changes in penguin populations tell us a great deal about the health of the Antarctic environment. In recent decades, some species have declined sharply in areas where the sea ice has melted earlier in the spring. Others, which prefer open water, have increased. By attaching small cameras and trackers to penguins, researchers can now follow exactly where the birds travel and what they eat throughout the year.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-18-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Why are penguins valuable to scientists?","options":["They live longer than other birds","Their population changes show the health of the Antarctic environment","They can carry large research equipment","They are easy to keep in laboratories"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-18-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：ペンギンは南極のシンボルとしてよく使われ、科学者にとって貴重な研究対象でもあります。ペンギンは繁殖にも、その下の小魚やエビを捕るにも海氷に依存しているため、ペンギンの個体数の変化は南極環境の健全性について多くを教えてくれます。近年、春に海氷が早く溶ける地域では、一部の種が急減しました。一方、開けた海を好む種は増えています。ペンギンに小型カメラや追跡装置を付けることで、研究者は鳥が1年を通してどこへ行き、何を食べるかを正確に追えるようになりました。","evidence":"changes in penguin populations tell us a great deal about the health of the Antarctic environment","explanation":"個体数変化が南極環境の健全性を示す指標になる点が、研究対象としての価値。","trapType":"価値の理由の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1B-19","num":19,"part":"1B","situation":"海洋生物学の授業紹介を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Let me introduce Marine Biology, a second-year course held in the spring term. The course combines lectures with practical fieldwork. The highlight is a one-day research trip by boat in May, when we will collect samples of sea water and small organisms and analyze them back in the laboratory. Because places on the boat are limited to twenty-five students, registration for this course closes earlier than usual, at the end of March. Students must also be able to swim, as we will work on the boat deck. The final grade is based on a laboratory report about the boat trip and a written exam in July.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-19-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Why does registration for this course close early?","options":["The laboratory is small","Places on the research boat are limited","The course is very popular with first-years","The exam is held in July"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-19-q.mp3","jaScript":"男：海洋生物学を紹介します。春学期に開かれる2年生向けの授業です。講義と実地調査を組み合わせます。目玉は5月の1日研究船トリップで、海水と小さな生物のサンプルを採取し、研究室に戻って分析します。船の定員が25名のため、この授業の登録は通常より早く、3月末に締め切ります。甲板で作業するため、泳げることも必要です。最終成績は、船トリップについての実験レポートと7月の筆記試験で決まります。","evidence":"Because places on the boat are limited to twenty-five students, registration for this course closes earlier than usual","explanation":"登録が早く締め切る理由は、研究船の定員25名という制約。","trapType":"理由の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1B-20","num":20,"part":"1B","situation":"大学のスポーツフェスティバルの案内を聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Sign-ups for the university sports festival are now open. This year's festival will be held on Saturday, June 7th, on the main sports ground. Students can enter as a class team or as a club team in events such as relay races, tug of war, and five-a-side soccer. Each student may join up to two events. Team captains must submit the entry form to the student office by May 16th. In case of rain, the festival will be postponed to the following Saturday, and an announcement will be made on the university website by seven in the morning on the day.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-20-t1.mp3"}],"question":"What must team captains do by May 16th?","options":["Pay the entry fee","Submit the entry form to the student office","Choose two events for each member","Check the weather announcement"],"answer":1,"audioQuestion":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1b-20-q.mp3","jaScript":"女：大学スポーツフェスティバルの申込が始まりました。今年のフェスティバルは6月7日（土）にメイングラウンドで開催されます。学生はクラスのチームまたはサークルのチームとして、リレー、綱引き、5人制サッカーなどの種目に参加できます。各学生は最大2種目まで参加可能です。チームのキャプテンは5月16日までに、学生課へ申込書を提出しなければなりません。雨天の場合は翌週の土曜に延期され、当日朝7時までに大学のウェブサイトでお知らせします。","evidence":"Team captains must submit the entry form to the student office by May 16th.","explanation":"キャプテンの義務は5月16日までの申込書提出。雨天案内は当日朝の話。","trapType":"期限つき義務の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-1C-21","num":21,"part":"1C","situation":"学生新聞の記者が好きな季節のアンケート結果について話しています。グラフを見ながら聞いてください。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Our student newspaper asked 200 students about their favorite season. Spring was the most popular, chosen by 40 percent. Summer and autumn were equal, at 25 percent each. Winter was chosen by only 10 percent of the students, mainly those who enjoy skiing.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1c-21-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Which chart correctly shows the survey results?","options":["Chart A","Chart B","Chart C","Chart D"],"chartOptions":[{"type":"bar","title":"Favorite Season","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"Spring","value":40},{"label":"Summer","value":25},{"label":"Autumn","value":10},{"label":"Winter","value":25}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Favorite Season","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"Spring","value":40},{"label":"Summer","value":25},{"label":"Autumn","value":25},{"label":"Winter","value":10}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Favorite Season","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"Spring","value":25},{"label":"Summer","value":40},{"label":"Autumn","value":25},{"label":"Winter","value":10}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Favorite Season","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"Spring","value":40},{"label":"Summer","value":10},{"label":"Autumn","value":25},{"label":"Winter","value":25}]}],"answer":1,"jaScript":"男：私たちの学生新聞は、200人の学生に好きな季節を聞きました。春が最も人気で、40％に選ばれました。夏と秋は同率で各25％でした。冬は10％だけで、主にスキーが好きな学生たちでした。","evidence":"Spring was the most popular, chosen by 40 percent. Summer and autumn were equal, at 25 percent each.","explanation":"正解は Chart B（Spring 40 / Summer 25 / Autumn 25 / Winter 10、合計100％）。夏と秋が同率である点が鍵。","trapType":"数値の入れ替え型グラフ"},{"id":"L-1C-22","num":22,"part":"1C","situation":"国際課の担当者が留学生数の推移について話しています。グラフを見ながら聞いてください。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Let me show you how our international student numbers have changed. In 2022, we had 120 international students. The number rose to 150 in 2023, then dipped slightly to 135 in 2024. This year, 2025, it has reached 180, our highest number ever.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1c-22-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Which chart correctly shows the international student numbers?","options":["Chart A","Chart B","Chart C","Chart D"],"chartOptions":[{"type":"line","title":"International Students","unit":"students","max":200,"labels":["2022","2023","2024","2025"],"values":[120,135,150,180]},{"type":"line","title":"International Students","unit":"students","max":200,"labels":["2022","2023","2024","2025"],"values":[120,150,180,135]},{"type":"line","title":"International Students","unit":"students","max":200,"labels":["2022","2023","2024","2025"],"values":[150,120,135,180]},{"type":"line","title":"International Students","unit":"students","max":200,"labels":["2022","2023","2024","2025"],"values":[120,150,135,180]}],"answer":3,"jaScript":"女：留学生数の変化をお見せします。2022年は120人でした。2023年には150人に増え、2024年には135人へ少し減りました。今年2025年は180人に達し、過去最高です。","evidence":"The number rose to 150 in 2023, then dipped slightly to 135 in 2024.","explanation":"正解は Chart D（120 / 150 / 135 / 180）。2024年に一度下がる点が鍵。","trapType":"数値の入れ替え型グラフ"},{"id":"L-1C-23","num":23,"part":"1C","situation":"語学センターの担当者が語学クラスの履修者数について話しています。グラフを見ながら聞いてください。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Here are this year's enrollments for our language classes. Spanish is the largest, with 90 students. Chinese comes next, with 75 students. French has 60 students, and German is the smallest, with 45 students.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1c-23-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Which chart correctly shows the enrollments?","options":["Chart A","Chart B","Chart C","Chart D"],"chartOptions":[{"type":"bar","title":"Language Class Enrollments","unit":"students","max":100,"items":[{"label":"Spanish","value":90},{"label":"French","value":60},{"label":"Chinese","value":75},{"label":"German","value":45}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Language Class Enrollments","unit":"students","max":100,"items":[{"label":"Spanish","value":90},{"label":"French","value":75},{"label":"Chinese","value":60},{"label":"German","value":45}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Language Class Enrollments","unit":"students","max":100,"items":[{"label":"Spanish","value":75},{"label":"French","value":60},{"label":"Chinese","value":90},{"label":"German","value":45}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Language Class Enrollments","unit":"students","max":100,"items":[{"label":"Spanish","value":90},{"label":"French","value":60},{"label":"Chinese","value":75},{"label":"German","value":60}]}],"answer":0,"jaScript":"男：今年の語学クラスの履修者数です。スペイン語が最大で90人。次が中国語で75人。フランス語は60人で、ドイツ語が最小の45人です。","evidence":"Spanish is the largest, with 90 students. Chinese comes next, with 75 students.","explanation":"正解は Chart A（Spanish 90 / French 60 / Chinese 75 / German 45）。フランス語と中国語の順序に注意。","trapType":"数値の入れ替え型グラフ"},{"id":"L-1C-24","num":24,"part":"1C","situation":"大学祭実行委員がウェブサイトのアクセス数について話しています。グラフを見ながら聞いてください。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Here are the visitor numbers for the university festival webpage last week. On Friday, we had 800 visitors. On Saturday, the number jumped to 2,400, and on Sunday it reached 3,100, the highest of the four days. On Monday, after the festival ended, it dropped back to 1,200.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1c-24-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Which chart correctly shows the visitor numbers?","options":["Chart A","Chart B","Chart C","Chart D"],"chartOptions":[{"type":"line","title":"Festival Webpage Visitors","unit":"visitors","max":3500,"labels":["Fri","Sat","Sun","Mon"],"values":[800,3100,2400,1200]},{"type":"line","title":"Festival Webpage Visitors","unit":"visitors","max":3500,"labels":["Fri","Sat","Sun","Mon"],"values":[800,2400,1200,3100]},{"type":"line","title":"Festival Webpage Visitors","unit":"visitors","max":3500,"labels":["Fri","Sat","Sun","Mon"],"values":[800,2400,3100,1200]},{"type":"line","title":"Festival Webpage Visitors","unit":"visitors","max":3500,"labels":["Fri","Sat","Sun","Mon"],"values":[1200,2400,3100,800]}],"answer":2,"jaScript":"女：先週の大学祭ウェブページの訪問者数です。金曜は800人でした。土曜には2,400人に跳ね上がり、日曜には3,100人で4日間の最高になりました。月曜は祭りが終わって1,200人に下がりました。","evidence":"On Saturday, the number jumped to 2,400, and on Sunday it reached 3,100, the highest of the four days.","explanation":"正解は Chart C（Fri 800 / Sat 2,400 / Sun 3,100 / Mon 1,200）。","trapType":"数値の入れ替え型グラフ"},{"id":"L-1C-25","num":25,"part":"1C","situation":"学生食堂の担当者が朝食についてのアンケート結果を話しています。グラフを見ながら聞いてください。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"We surveyed students about where they eat breakfast. Forty-five percent eat breakfast at home. Twenty-five percent buy something at a convenience store. Twenty percent use the cafeteria, and the remaining ten percent usually skip breakfast altogether.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-1c-25-t1.mp3"}],"question":"Which chart correctly shows the survey results?","options":["Chart A","Chart B","Chart C","Chart D"],"chartOptions":[{"type":"bar","title":"Where Students Eat Breakfast","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"At Home","value":45},{"label":"Cafeteria","value":25},{"label":"Convenience Store","value":20},{"label":"Skip","value":10}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Where Students Eat Breakfast","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"At Home","value":45},{"label":"Cafeteria","value":20},{"label":"Convenience Store","value":25},{"label":"Skip","value":10}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Where Students Eat Breakfast","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"At Home","value":45},{"label":"Cafeteria","value":20},{"label":"Convenience Store","value":25},{"label":"Skip","value":15}]},{"type":"bar","title":"Where Students Eat Breakfast","unit":"%","max":50,"items":[{"label":"At Home","value":25},{"label":"Cafeteria","value":20},{"label":"Convenience Store","value":45},{"label":"Skip","value":10}]}],"answer":1,"jaScript":"男：学生に朝食をどこで食べるかアンケートしました。45％が自宅で食べます。25％がコンビニで何か買います。20％が食堂を利用し、残りの10％は普段朝食を抜きます。","evidence":"Twenty-five percent buy something at a convenience store. Twenty percent use the cafeteria","explanation":"正解は Chart B（At Home 45 / Cafeteria 20 / Convenience Store 25 / Skip 10、合計100％）。食堂とコンビニの順序に注意。","trapType":"数値の入れ替え型グラフ"}],"p2":[{"setId":"L-2A-SET1","part":"2A","title":"Set 1: Planning a Class Trip to a Museum","situation":"2人の学生がクラスの美術館見学旅行について計画しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Our art history class is going to the national museum next month. The professor asked us to organize the trip. First, we need to choose a date.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The museum is closed on Mondays, and our class meets on Thursdays. How about Friday the 14th? Most students are free that day.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"The 14th works. Now, transport. The train takes forty minutes, but we'd have to change trains twice. A chartered bus is simpler, though more expensive.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"There are thirty of us. If we split the bus cost, it comes to 900 yen per person, only 200 yen more than the train. I think the bus is worth it.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Agreed. Let's book the bus. Next, the museum tickets. Students get in free with their ID, but the special exhibition costs 500 yen extra.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The professor said the special exhibition is the main purpose of the trip, so we should collect the 500 yen from everyone in advance.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t6.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"OK. I'll collect the money — 900 for the bus and 500 for the exhibition — by the end of next week. What about lunch?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t7.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The museum restaurant is expensive and small. There is a park nearby, so let's suggest that students bring their own lunch and eat there if the weather is fine.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t8.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Good idea. And if it rains, we can use the free rest area inside the museum. I'll write that in the announcement.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t9.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Perfect. So: Friday the 14th, chartered bus, 1,400 yen collected per person, and bring-your-own lunch. I'll make the bus reservation today.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set1-t10.mp3"}],"jaScript":"男：僕たちの美術史のクラスは来月、国立美術館に行くんだ。先生に旅行の手配を頼まれた。まず日付を決めないと。\n女：美術館は月曜休館で、授業は木曜だね。14日の金曜はどう？ほとんどの学生が空いている日だよ。\n男：14日でいいよ。次に交通手段。電車は40分だけど、乗り換えが2回ある。貸切バスは簡単だけど高い。\n女：30人いるから、バス代を割り勘にすれば1人900円で、電車より200円高いだけ。バスの価値はあると思う。\n男：賛成。バスを予約しよう。次に美術館のチケット。学生はIDで無料だけど、特別展は別途500円かかる。\n女：先生は特別展が旅行の主目的だと言っていたから、500円は全員から事前に集めるべきだね。\n男：OK。バス900円と特別展500円を来週末までに集めるよ。昼食はどうする？\n女：美術館のレストランは高くて小さい。近くに公園があるから、天気がよければ各自お弁当を持ってきてそこで食べるよう提案しよう。\n男：いいね。雨なら美術館内の無料休憩エリアが使える。案内に書いておくよ。\n女：完璧。まとめると、14日金曜、貸切バス、1人1,400円集金、昼食は持参。バスの予約は今日しておくね。","questions":[{"id":"L-2A-1-1","num":26,"question":"Why was the bus chosen instead of the train?","options":["It is cheaper than the train","It is simpler, and the extra cost per person is small","It is faster than the train","The train does not run on Fridays"],"answer":1,"evidence":"A chartered bus is simpler, though more expensive.","explanation":"バスは割高だが乗り換えなしで簡単で、1人あたりの差額が200円と小さいため選ばれた。安いからではない。","trapType":"理由の正確な聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2A-1-2","num":27,"question":"How much money will be collected from each student?","options":["500 yen","900 yen","1,400 yen","200 yen"],"answer":2,"evidence":"I'll collect the money — 900 for the bus and 500 for the exhibition","explanation":"バス900円＋特別展500円＝1人1,400円を集金する。","trapType":"合計金額の計算"},{"id":"L-2A-1-3","num":28,"question":"What will students do for lunch if it rains?","options":["Eat at the museum restaurant","Use the free rest area inside the museum","Return to the university early","Buy lunch at the park shop"],"answer":1,"evidence":"if it rains, we can use the free rest area inside the museum","explanation":"雨天時は美術館内の無料休憩エリアで持参の昼食を食べる計画。","trapType":"条件分岐の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2A-SET2","part":"2A","title":"Set 2: Booking a Homestay for Spring Break","situation":"学生が大学の旅行デスクで、春休みのホームステイについて相談しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"Hello, I'd like to arrange a homestay in Australia during spring break. I saw your program brochure.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Welcome. Our homestay program is in Brisbane. Stays are two weeks or four weeks. Which are you thinking of?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Four weeks is too long for my budget. Two weeks, please. Does the price include meals?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Yes, breakfast and dinner on weekdays are included. On weekends, most students eat out or cook with their host family.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"I see. Is there a language school nearby? I want to take English classes in the mornings.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"All our host families live within twenty minutes of the partner language school by bus. Classes are optional and cost extra.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t6.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"I'll decide about the classes later. What is the application deadline?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t7.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"For spring break departures, applications close on December 20th, because we need time to match students with families.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t8.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"That's soon. What do I need to apply?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t9.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"The application form, a copy of your passport, and a short essay in English about why you want to do a homestay — about 300 words.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t10.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"All right. I'll write the essay this week and submit everything by Friday.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set2-t11.mp3"}],"jaScript":"女：こんにちは、春休みにオーストラリアでホームステイを手配したいです。プログラムのパンフレットを見ました。\n男：ようこそ。私たちのホームステイプログラムはブリスベンです。滞在は2週間か4週間です。どちらをお考えですか？\n女：4週間は予算的に長すぎます。2週間でお願いします。料金に食事は含まれますか？\n男：はい、平日の朝食と夕食が含まれます。週末は、多くの学生が外食するか、ホストファミリーと一緒に料理します。\n女：なるほど。近くに語学学校はありますか？午前中に英語の授業を受けたいんです。\n男：ホストファミリーは全て、提携語学学校からバスで20分以内に住んでいます。授業は任意で別料金です。\n女：授業については後で決めます。申込の締切はいつですか？\n男：春休み出発分は、学生とファミリーのマッチングに時間が必要なため、12月20日に締め切ります。\n女：すぐですね。申込には何が必要ですか？\n男：申込書、パスポートのコピー、そしてホームステイをしたい理由についての英語の短いエッセイ（約300語）です。\n女：分かりました。今週エッセイを書いて、金曜までに全部提出します。","questions":[{"id":"L-2A-2-1","num":29,"question":"Why does the woman choose the two-week stay?","options":["The four-week stay is fully booked","The four-week stay is too expensive for her budget","Her classes start in two weeks","The language school only offers two weeks"],"answer":1,"evidence":"Four weeks is too long for my budget. Two weeks, please.","explanation":"4週間は予算の都合で長すぎるため、2週間を選んだ。","trapType":"理由の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2A-2-2","num":30,"question":"What is included in the homestay price?","options":["All meals, every day","Breakfast and dinner on weekdays","English classes in the mornings","Bus passes for four weeks"],"answer":1,"evidence":"breakfast and dinner on weekdays are included","explanation":"含まれるのは平日の朝食と夕食。週末の食事と語学授業は含まれない。","trapType":"含まれる範囲の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2A-2-3","num":31,"question":"What must the woman submit by December 20th?","options":["Only the application form","The form, a passport copy, and a short English essay","The form and the full payment","A letter from her professor"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The application form, a copy of your passport, and a short essay in English","explanation":"申込書類は申込書・パスポートのコピー・約300語の英語エッセイの3点。","trapType":"必要書類の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2A-SET3","part":"2A","title":"Set 3: Changing a Thesis Topic","situation":"学生が教授の研究室で、卒業論文のテーマ変更について相談しています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Professor, thank you for your time. I would like to ask about changing my thesis topic.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"You were going to write about tourism in your hometown, correct? What happened?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I started the research, but I found that very little data is available. The town's tourism office closed last year, and their records were lost.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"That is a serious problem. A thesis needs data you can actually analyze. What topic are you considering instead?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I'd like to study how foreign tourists use social media to choose restaurants in Kyoto. I can collect real posts and reviews as data.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t5.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"That sounds workable, and the data is accessible. But the topic is broad. You should limit it — for example, to one district of Kyoto, or to one type of restaurant.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t6.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I'll limit it to traditional sweet shops in one district. I know several owners there from my part-time job.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t7.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Good — personal connections will help your interviews too. Now, the deadline for topic changes is the end of this month. Please submit the new proposal form by then.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t8.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"I'll prepare the proposal this week. Should the proposal include my research schedule?","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t9.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Yes, include a monthly schedule up to the submission date in January. And list the data sources you plan to use. Bring me a draft next week if you want feedback before the deadline.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2a-set3-t10.mp3"}],"jaScript":"男：先生、お時間ありがとうございます。卒論のテーマ変更について相談したいです。\n女：あなたのテーマは故郷の観光でしたね。何があったのですか？\n男：調査を始めたのですが、使えるデータがほとんどないことが分かりました。町の観光事務所が昨年閉鎖され、記録も失われていたんです。\n女：それは深刻な問題ですね。論文には、実際に分析できるデータが必要です。代わりにどんなテーマを考えていますか？\n男：外国人観光客がSNSをどう使って京都の飲食店を選ぶかを研究したいです。実際の投稿やレビューをデータとして集められます。\n女：それは実現可能そうだし、データにもアクセスできます。ただテーマが広いので、絞るべきです。例えば京都の1つの地区、あるいは1つのタイプの飲食店に。\n男：1つの地区の伝統的な甘味処に絞ります。アルバイトの関係で、そこの店主を何人か知っています。\n女：いいですね。人脈はインタビューにも役立ちます。テーマ変更の締切は今月末です。それまでに新しい提案書を提出してください。\n男：今週提案書を用意します。提案書には研究スケジュールも入れるべきですか？\n女：はい、1月の提出日までの月ごとのスケジュールを含めてください。それと、使う予定のデータ源の一覧も。締切前にフィードバックが欲しければ、来週下書きを持ってきて。","questions":[{"id":"L-2A-3-1","num":32,"question":"Why does the man want to change his thesis topic?","options":["His professor rejected the original topic","Almost no data is available for the original topic","He lost interest in tourism","His hometown is too far away to visit"],"answer":1,"evidence":"I found that very little data is available. The town's tourism office closed last year, and their records were lost.","explanation":"観光事務所の閉鎖と記録の喪失で、元のテーマには分析できるデータがほとんどないため変更を希望した。","trapType":"理由の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2A-3-2","num":33,"question":"What advice does the professor give about the new topic?","options":["Compare several cities","Limit the topic to one district or one type of restaurant","Focus on Japanese tourists instead","Use only interview data"],"answer":1,"evidence":"the topic is broad. You should limit it — for example, to one district of Kyoto, or to one type of restaurant.","explanation":"新テーマは広すぎるため、1地区または1タイプの飲食店に絞るよう助言された。学生は地区＋甘味処の両方で絞った。","trapType":"助言の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2A-3-3","num":34,"question":"What must the new proposal include?","options":["A monthly schedule and a list of data sources","A summary of the old research","Letters from restaurant owners","A budget for travel to Kyoto"],"answer":0,"evidence":"include a monthly schedule up to the submission date in January. And list the data sources you plan to use.","explanation":"提案書には1月提出までの月次スケジュールと、使用予定のデータ源リストを含める必要がある。","trapType":"必須項目の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2B-SET1","part":"2B","title":"Set 1: Astronomy — The Life Cycle of Stars","situation":"天文学の講義で、星の生涯について聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Today we will follow the life of a star, from its birth to its death. Stars are born inside huge clouds of gas and dust called nebulae. When part of such a cloud collapses under its own gravity, the center becomes hotter and denser until nuclear fusion begins — hydrogen atoms join to form helium, releasing enormous energy. At that moment, a star is born and starts to shine.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set1-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"For most of its life, a star is stable, because the outward pressure from fusion balances the inward pull of gravity. Our sun is in this stage now, and it has enough hydrogen to continue for about another five billion years. But no star shines forever. When the hydrogen in the core runs out, the balance is broken and the star begins to change dramatically.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set1-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"What happens next depends mainly on the star's mass. A star like the sun will expand into a red giant, swallowing the inner planets, and then slowly shed its outer layers. The remaining core becomes a white dwarf, a small, dense object that cools over billions of years. There is no explosion; the death of a small star is quiet.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set1-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"A much heavier star dies very differently. Its core collapses suddenly, and the outer parts explode outward in a supernova — an explosion so bright that it can outshine an entire galaxy for a few weeks. The material thrown into space contains heavy elements such as carbon, oxygen, and iron. The remaining core may become a neutron star or, if it is heavy enough, a black hole.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set1-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"This is why astronomers say we are made of star material. The carbon in our bodies and the iron in our blood were created inside stars and scattered by supernovae long before the Earth existed. In the next lecture, we will look at how astronomers measure the distance to stars. Please review the diagram of the star life cycle in your textbook before then.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set1-t5.mp3"}],"jaScript":"男：今日は、星の誕生から死までの生涯をたどります。星は、星雲と呼ばれるガスと塵の巨大な雲の中で生まれます。雲の一部が自身の重力で崩壊すると、中心は熱く高密度になり、核融合が始まります。水素原子が結びついてヘリウムになり、膨大なエネルギーを放出します。その瞬間、星が生まれ、輝き始めます。\n生涯の大半、星は安定しています。核融合による外向きの圧力と、重力による内向きの引力が釣り合っているからです。私たちの太陽は今この段階にあり、あと約50億年続けられるだけの水素があります。しかし、永遠に輝く星はありません。中心の水素が尽きると釣り合いが崩れ、星は劇的に変化し始めます。\nその後どうなるかは、主に星の質量で決まります。太陽のような星は赤色巨星に膨張して内側の惑星を飲み込み、その後ゆっくりと外層を放出します。残った中心部は白色矮星となり、何十億年もかけて冷えていきます。爆発はありません。小さな星の死は静かです。\nはるかに重い星は全く違う死に方をします。中心部が突然崩壊し、外側が超新星爆発で吹き飛びます。その明るさは、数週間、銀河全体を上回るほどです。宇宙に放出される物質には、炭素、酸素、鉄といった重い元素が含まれます。残った中心部は中性子星に、十分重ければブラックホールになります。\nだから天文学者は「私たちは星の物質でできている」と言います。私たちの体の炭素や血液の鉄は、地球が存在するずっと前に、星の内部で作られ超新星でまき散らされたものです。次回は、天文学者が星までの距離をどう測るかを見ます。それまでに教科書の星の生涯の図を復習しておいてください。","questions":[{"id":"L-2B-1-1","num":35,"question":"What event marks the birth of a star?","options":["A supernova explosion","The start of nuclear fusion in the core","The formation of a black hole","The collapse of a white dwarf"],"answer":1,"evidence":"until nuclear fusion begins — hydrogen atoms join to form helium, releasing enormous energy. At that moment, a star is born","explanation":"星の誕生は、中心で核融合が始まる瞬間と定義されている。","trapType":"定義の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-1-2","num":36,"question":"Why is a star stable for most of its life?","options":["Its hydrogen never decreases","Fusion pressure outward balances gravity inward","It slowly gains mass from space","Its core keeps cooling down"],"answer":1,"evidence":"the outward pressure from fusion balances the inward pull of gravity","explanation":"安定の理由は、核融合の外向き圧力と重力の内向き引力の釣り合い。","trapType":"理由の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-1-3","num":37,"question":"What mainly determines how a star dies?","options":["Its age","Its distance from Earth","Its mass","Its color"],"answer":2,"evidence":"What happens next depends mainly on the star's mass.","explanation":"死に方を決める主因は質量。小さい星は静かに白色矮星に、重い星は超新星爆発へ。","trapType":"決定要因の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-1-4","num":38,"question":"Why do astronomers say humans are made of star material?","options":["The sun created the Earth directly","Heavy elements in our bodies were made inside stars and scattered by supernovae","Stars and humans have the same temperature","The Earth was once part of a star's surface"],"answer":1,"evidence":"The carbon in our bodies and the iron in our blood were created inside stars and scattered by supernovae","explanation":"体内の炭素や鉄は星の内部で作られ、超新星でまき散らされた元素だから。","trapType":"理由の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2B-SET2","part":"2B","title":"Set 2: Sociology — The Growth of Cities","situation":"社会学の講義で、都市化について聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"For most of human history, the great majority of people lived in the countryside. As the chart shows, in 1950 only 30 percent of the world's population lived in cities. By 1980 the share had risen to 39 percent, by 2000 to 47 percent, and in 2020 it reached 56 percent. Sometime around 2007, the world passed a turning point: for the first time, more people lived in cities than in rural areas.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set2-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Why did people move? The main pull was economic. Factories and offices offered wages that farming could not match, and cities provided schools, hospitals, and entertainment. At the same time, machines reduced the number of workers needed on farms, pushing people to leave. Sociologists call these pull factors and push factors, and both worked together throughout the twentieth century.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set2-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Urbanization brought real benefits: higher incomes, better services, and new ideas that arise when many people live closely together. But it also created problems. Housing in popular cities became expensive, trains and roads grew crowded, and some neighborhoods lacked clean water or waste collection, especially in rapidly growing cities in developing countries.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set2-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The trend is not the same everywhere. In rich countries, urbanization has slowed, and some people are even moving back to smaller towns, especially since remote work became common. In contrast, cities in Africa and parts of Asia are still growing fast. By 2050, researchers expect about two-thirds of humanity to live in cities, with most of that growth in Africa and Asia.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set2-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"So the key question for your generation is not whether cities will grow, but how they can grow well — with housing, transport, and services that keep pace. Next week, we will examine one city that managed rapid growth successfully, and one that struggled. Please read the case studies in chapter six before the class.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set2-t5.mp3"}],"stimulusChart":{"type":"line","title":"Share of World Population Living in Cities","sub":"Percentage of total population","unit":"%","max":60,"labels":["1950","1980","2000","2020"],"values":[30,39,47,56]},"jaScript":"女：人類史の大半の期間、大多数の人々は田舎に住んでいました。グラフの通り、1950年には世界人口の30％しか都市に住んでいませんでした。1980年には39％、2000年には47％に上がり、2020年には56％に達しました。2007年頃、世界は転機を迎えました。初めて、田舎より都市に住む人の方が多くなったのです。\nなぜ人々は移動したのでしょうか。主な引力は経済でした。工場やオフィスは農業では得られない賃金を提供し、都市は学校、病院、娯楽を提供しました。同時に、機械化で農場に必要な労働者は減り、人々を押し出す力になりました。社会学者はこれらをプル要因とプッシュ要因と呼び、20世紀を通して両方が一緒に働きました。\n都市化は本当の恩恵をもたらしました。所得の上昇、より良いサービス、そして多くの人が密接に暮らすことで生まれる新しいアイデアです。しかし問題も生みました。人気都市の住宅は高くなり、電車や道路は混雑し、特に発展途上国の急成長都市では、清潔な水やごみ収集のない地区もありました。\nこの傾向はどこでも同じではありません。豊かな国では都市化は鈍化し、特にリモートワークが普及してからは、小さな町へ戻る人もいます。対照的に、アフリカやアジアの一部の都市は今も急速に成長しています。2050年までに、人類の約3分の2が都市に住み、その成長の大半はアフリカとアジアで起きると研究者は予測しています。\nつまり皆さんの世代にとっての問いは、都市が成長するかどうかではなく、住宅、交通、サービスが追いつく形でどう良く成長できるかです。来週は、急成長をうまく管理した都市と、苦労した都市を検討します。第6章のケーススタディを読んできてください。","questions":[{"id":"L-2B-2-1","num":39,"question":"According to the chart, what percentage of the world's population lived in cities in 2020?","options":["39 percent","47 percent","56 percent","30 percent"],"answer":2,"evidence":"in 2020 it reached 56 percent","explanation":"グラフと本文の両方で2020年は56％。","trapType":"グラフ読み取り"},{"id":"L-2B-2-2","num":40,"question":"What happened around 2007?","options":["The first megacity appeared","City populations first exceeded rural populations","Remote work became common","Urbanization stopped in rich countries"],"answer":1,"evidence":"for the first time, more people lived in cities than in rural areas","explanation":"2007年頃、都市人口が初めて農村人口を上回った。","trapType":"転機の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-2-3","num":41,"question":"Which is described as a push factor for urbanization?","options":["Higher wages in factories","Better hospitals in cities","Machines reducing the need for farm workers","More entertainment in cities"],"answer":2,"evidence":"machines reduced the number of workers needed on farms, pushing people to leave","explanation":"プッシュ要因は農業機械化による労働需要の減少。賃金や病院はプル要因。","trapType":"分類の聞き分け"},{"id":"L-2B-2-4","num":42,"question":"Where is most future urban growth expected?","options":["Europe and North America","Africa and Asia","South America","Small towns in rich countries"],"answer":1,"evidence":"most of that growth in Africa and Asia","explanation":"2050年に向けた都市成長の大半はアフリカとアジアで起きると予測されている。","trapType":"予測の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2B-SET3","part":"2B","title":"Set 3: Chemistry — Acids and Bases in Daily Life","situation":"化学の講義で、日常生活の中の酸と塩基について聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"M","text":"Today we will talk about acids and bases, which sound like laboratory chemicals but are actually part of your kitchen and bathroom. An acid is a substance that releases hydrogen ions in water, while a base accepts them. Chemists measure this with the pH scale, from zero to fourteen. Below seven is acidic, seven is neutral, and above seven is basic, or alkaline. Pure water, at exactly seven, sits in the middle.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set3-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"Common acids include the citric acid in lemons, which gives them their sour taste, and the acetic acid in vinegar. Your stomach uses a much stronger acid, hydrochloric acid, to digest food and kill bacteria. Bases are equally familiar: baking soda is a weak base used in cooking and cleaning, and soap is mildly basic, which is why it can feel slippery and why it removes grease, since grease breaks down more easily in basic solutions.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set3-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"The most useful property of acids and bases is that they neutralize each other. When an acid and a base are mixed in the right amounts, they produce water and a salt, and the result is closer to neutral. This is exactly how antacid medicine works: it contains a weak base that neutralizes part of the excess acid in your stomach. Farmers use the same idea when they add lime, a base, to soil that has become too acidic for their crops.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set3-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"However, strong acids and bases are dangerous and must be handled carefully. The strength depends not only on pH but also on concentration. A concentrated acid can burn skin and damage eyes even in a small drop. In the laboratory, we always add acid to water, never water to acid, because the mixture can heat up and splash. Safety glasses are required in all our practical classes for this reason.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set3-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"M","text":"To summarize: acids and bases are defined by hydrogen ions, measured on the pH scale, and they neutralize each other — a principle used in medicine, farming, and cooking. Next time, we will do a practical experiment using a natural indicator made from red cabbage, which changes color depending on pH. Please bring your lab coats and read the safety instructions in advance.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set3-t5.mp3"}],"jaScript":"男：今日は酸と塩基について話します。実験室の化学物質のように聞こえますが、実は台所や浴室にもあるものです。酸は水の中で水素イオンを放出する物質で、塩基はそれを受け取ります。化学者はpHスケール（0から14）でこれを測ります。7未満は酸性、7は中性、7より大きいと塩基性（アルカリ性）です。純水はちょうど7で真ん中にあります。\n身近な酸には、レモンの酸っぱさの元であるクエン酸や、酢の酢酸があります。胃はもっと強い酸、塩酸を使って食物を消化し細菌を殺します。塩基も同様に身近です。重曹は調理や掃除に使われる弱い塩基で、石鹸は弱塩基性なのでぬるぬる感じ、油脂が塩基性の溶液で分解しやすいため油汚れを落とせます。\n酸と塩基の最も便利な性質は、互いに中和することです。適切な量を混ぜると水と塩ができ、結果は中性に近づきます。胃薬はまさにこの仕組みで、弱い塩基が胃の余分な酸の一部を中和します。農家も同じ考えで、作物には酸性すぎる土壌に塩基である石灰を加えます。\nただし、強い酸や塩基は危険で、注意して扱わなければなりません。強さはpHだけでなく濃度にも依存します。濃い酸は一滴でも皮膚をやけどさせ、目を傷つけます。実験室では、混合物が熱を持って飛び散ることがあるため、常に「水に酸を加える」のであって、その逆はしません。実習では保護メガネが必須なのはこのためです。\nまとめると、酸と塩基は水素イオンで定義され、pHスケールで測られ、互いに中和します。この原理は医療、農業、調理に使われます。次回は、pHによって色が変わる赤キャベツ製の天然指示薬を使った実験をします。白衣を持参し、安全の指示を事前に読んでおいてください。","questions":[{"id":"L-2B-3-1","num":43,"question":"On the pH scale, what does a value above seven mean?","options":["The substance is acidic","The substance is neutral","The substance is basic","The substance is pure water"],"answer":2,"evidence":"Below seven is acidic, seven is neutral, and above seven is basic, or alkaline.","explanation":"pH7超は塩基性（アルカリ性）。7は中性で純水が該当。","trapType":"定義の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-3-2","num":44,"question":"How does antacid medicine work?","options":["It adds more acid to the stomach","A weak base in it neutralizes excess stomach acid","It covers the stomach with a protective layer","It kills bacteria in the stomach"],"answer":1,"evidence":"it contains a weak base that neutralizes part of the excess acid in your stomach","explanation":"胃薬は弱塩基が余分な胃酸を中和する仕組み。細菌を殺すのは胃酸自体の役割。","trapType":"仕組みの聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-3-3","num":45,"question":"Why must acid always be added to water, and not the other way around?","options":["Water is cheaper than acid","The mixture can heat up and splash","Acid loses its strength in water","Water changes color when acid is added"],"answer":1,"evidence":"we always add acid to water, never water to acid, because the mixture can heat up and splash","explanation":"逆に加えると発熱して飛び散る危険があるため。","trapType":"安全規則の理由"},{"id":"L-2B-3-4","num":46,"question":"What will students use in the next experiment?","options":["A synthetic chemical indicator","An indicator made from red cabbage","Lemon juice and vinegar","A digital pH meter"],"answer":1,"evidence":"we will do a practical experiment using a natural indicator made from red cabbage","explanation":"次回の実験では赤キャベツ製の天然指示薬を使う。","trapType":"次回内容の聞き取り"}]},{"setId":"L-2B-SET4","part":"2B","title":"Set 4: Biology — The Migration of Monarch Butterflies","situation":"生物学の講義で、オオカバマダラの渡りについて聞いています。","turns":[{"speaker":"W","text":"The monarch butterfly performs one of the most remarkable migrations in the insect world. Every autumn, millions of monarchs from North America fly south — some traveling nearly four thousand kilometers — to mountain forests in central Mexico. Look at the chart, which shows estimates of the wintering population in Mexico. In 2000, about 120 million butterflies arrived. By 2010, the number had fallen to 65 million, and in 2015 it reached a low of 42 million. By 2024, it had recovered somewhat, to 55 million.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set4-t1.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The most astonishing fact is that no single butterfly makes the whole round trip. Monarchs live only a few weeks as adults, so the journey south is completed by one generation, but the return north in spring takes three or four generations, each flying part of the way and laying eggs along the route. How the final generation finds the same Mexican forests that its great-grandparents left remains one of the great puzzles of biology.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set4-t2.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"Why have the numbers fallen? Researchers point to three main causes. First, the loss of milkweed, the only plant on which monarchs lay their eggs, as farming has expanded in North America. Second, logging and storms have damaged the Mexican forests where the butterflies spend the winter. Third, extreme weather — droughts and sudden cold — has killed large numbers during migration in some years.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set4-t3.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The partial recovery by 2024 suggests that conservation can work. In Mexico, protected areas around the wintering forests have been expanded, and logging has been reduced. In the United States and Canada, campaigns encourage people to plant milkweed in gardens and along roads. Schoolchildren even raise monarchs in classrooms and release them, which builds public support for protecting the species.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set4-t4.mp3"},{"speaker":"W","text":"The monarch's story shows how a species can depend on habitats in several countries at once, so protecting it requires international cooperation. Next week, we will compare the monarch with other migrating animals, including birds that cross entire oceans. For your assignment, write a short report explaining which of the three causes of decline you consider most serious, and why.","audio":"/teap/audio/s3-l-2b-set4-t5.mp3"}],"stimulusChart":{"type":"line","title":"Monarch Butterflies Wintering in Mexico","sub":"Estimated number of butterflies","unit":"millions","max":140,"labels":["2000","2010","2015","2024"],"values":[120,65,42,55]},"jaScript":"女：オオカバマダラは、昆虫の世界で最も驚くべき渡りの1つを行います。毎年秋、北アメリカから何百万ものオオカバマダラが南へ飛び、中にはほぼ4000キロを移動してメキシコ中央部の山林にたどり着きます。メキシコで越冬する個体数の推定を示したグラフを見てください。2000年には約1億2000万頭が到着しました。2010年には6500万頭まで減り、2015年には4200万頭という最低値に達しました。2024年には5500万頭までやや回復しました。\n最も驚くべき事実は、1頭の蝶が往復の全行程を移動するわけではないことです。成虫の寿命は数週間しかないため、南への旅は1世代で完結しますが、春の北への帰還は3〜4世代がかかり、各世代が途中まで飛んで卵を産みます。最後の世代が、曾祖父母が離れたのと同じメキシコの森をどうやって見つけるのかは、生物学の大いなる謎の1つです。\nなぜ数が減ったのでしょうか。研究者は3つの主因を挙げます。第1に、北アメリカで農業が拡大し、オオカバマダラが卵を産む唯一の植物であるトウワタが失われたこと。第2に、蝶が冬を過ごすメキシコの森が伐採や嵐で傷ついたこと。第3に、干ばつや突然の寒さといった異常気象が、渡りの途中で大量の蝶を死なせたことです。\n2024年までの部分的な回復は、保護活動が機能しうることを示唆しています。メキシコでは越冬林周辺の保護区が拡大され、伐採が減りました。アメリカとカナダでは、庭や道路沿いにトウワタを植えるキャンペーンが行われています。子どもたちが教室でオオカバマダラを育てて放す活動もあり、種の保護への支持を広げています。\nオオカバマダラの物語は、1つの種が複数の国の生息地に同時に依存しうること、そしてその保護には国際協力が必要なことを示しています。来週は、オオカバマダラと、海を渡る鳥など他の渡り動物を比較します。課題として、減少の3つの原因のうちどれが最も深刻と考えるか、理由とともに短いレポートを書いてください。","questions":[{"id":"L-2B-4-1","num":47,"question":"According to the chart, in which year was the wintering population the lowest?","options":["2000","2010","2015","2024"],"answer":2,"evidence":"in 2015 it reached a low of 42 million","explanation":"グラフと本文の両方で、最低は2015年の4200万頭。","trapType":"グラフ読み取り"},{"id":"L-2B-4-2","num":48,"question":"What is unusual about the monarch's return journey north?","options":["The butterflies fly only at night","It is completed by several generations, not one","The butterflies do not stop to eat","A different species leads the way"],"answer":1,"evidence":"the return north in spring takes three or four generations, each flying part of the way","explanation":"北への帰還は3〜4世代がリレー形式で完遂する点が特異。","trapType":"要点の聞き取り"},{"id":"L-2B-4-3","num":49,"question":"Which is NOT mentioned as a cause of the decline?","options":["Loss of milkweed plants","Damage to the Mexican wintering forests","Extreme weather during migration","Hunting by collectors in Mexico"],"answer":3,"evidence":"the loss of milkweed, the only plant on which monarchs lay their eggs","explanation":"挙げられた3因はトウワタの喪失、越冬林の損傷、異常気象。コレクターの捕獲は言及されていない。","trapType":"NOT型"},{"id":"L-2B-4-4","num":50,"question":"What does the lecturer ask students to do for the assignment?","options":["Plant milkweed in the university garden","Write a report on the most serious cause of the decline","Count butterflies during the winter","Visit a protected area in Mexico"],"answer":1,"evidence":"write a short report explaining which of the three causes of decline you consider most serious, and why","explanation":"課題は、減少の3因のうち最も深刻なものとその理由を書く短いレポート。","trapType":"課題内容の聞き取り"}]}]};
